### RECORD ANT-003
**Motif:** Maps showing Terra Australis — 15th–18th century cartographic tradition
**Region:** Europe (various publishing centers)
**Period:** 1500–1820
**Source type:** Historical maps — published cartography
**Evidence class:** A (maps exist as artifacts)

**Notes:**
Later medieval references to Terra Australis Incognita, or "Unknown Land of the South," would elaborate on these old-fangled theories, and maps drawn between the 15th and 18th centuries often included it at the bottom. None of these names or renderings truly referred to the Antarctica we know today, since it would be hundreds of years before the continent was actually seen, but they did fuel a geographical myth that persisted for centuries.

**Specific map record — Petrus Plancius, 1590:**
Originally completed in 1590, Plancius's world map depicts Antarctica as nearly reaching the southern end of South America. It also shows the continent extending north almost to New Guinea and Java, reaching far into the Indian Ocean and terminating relatively close to the southern shores of Africa.

**⚠️ This is exactly the kind of entry your "map at the time" methodology is built for.** These maps were projections of imagination + theoretical necessity + distorted reports, not observation. They predate the actual sighting of Antarctica by 230+ years and yet shaped every subsequent exploratory assumption. Mapping the lineage of these drawn coastlines — which maps copied which — is a project in itself.

**Cartographic evolution note:**
Gilles and his son Didier, two of the 18th century's most well-known French cartographers, produced some of the last maps to predate the confirmed 1820 sighting of Antarctica. One of their most extensive maps, the 1749 Mappe-Monde, also omits Terra Australis. The landmass is replaced with the unknown area of Terres Antarctiques, translating simply to "Antarctic Lands."

